= Fusil o pueblo

It is the argument that has broken every armed movement on the continent, and it has arrived in your columns wearing the faces of people you trust. On one side the *militaristas*: the war is the vanguard, the action is the recruiter, every operation teaches the masses more than a decade of pamphlets — strike, strike, strike, and let the strikes summon the army the people are waiting for. On the other the *línea de masas*: the gun without the base is a suicide with an ideology, the column that outruns the villages that feed it is already a corpse that has not been told; build slow, root deep, and let the war grow from the people instead of ahead of them.

Both sides can point to graves that prove them right. The decision is not academic — it will set the tempo of the whole war, and the losers of the argument do not always stay.

- @linea_militarista: The military line. The action is the argument; accelerate, and make the war impossible to ignore.
- @linea_masas: The mass line. The base is the war; slow down, root deeper, and outlast them.
- @linea_equilibrio: Hold the synthesis — neither the *foco* nor the pamphlet, and spend authority keeping both currents in one army.

@linea_militarista
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: linea_guerra += 12; parque += 2; pres_guerrero += 2; pres_valle += 2; sup_peasants -= 2; disciplina -= 3; moral_columnas += 4

The columns are unleashed and the war gets loud: barracks probed, convoys hit, the communiqués arriving faster than the state can bury them, and for a season the young flock to a movement that is visibly *doing something*. It also cuts the war loose from its slow roots — the villages that fed it become audiences instead of participants, the army follows the noise straight to the noise-makers, and somewhere a mass-line cadre packs a bag, having lost the argument and, he suspects, the war. Speed has a bill. It is always paid later, and always by more people than signed for it.

@linea_masas
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: linea_guerra -= 12; disciplina += 4; sup_peasants += 3; sup_colonos += 2; parque -= 1; moral_columnas -= 2

The order goes out that no one wants to give in a war: *slow down.* Fewer operations, more schools; fewer ambushes, more assemblies; the column dissolving back into the villages it came from until the line between the guerrilla and the people is a thing the army cannot find because it does not exist. It is correct and it is agonizing — the militants who joined to fight spend the season teaching arithmetic, some of them leave for groups that will let them shoot, and the war grows so slowly it looks, to the impatient, like surrender. It is not. It is the only version of this that has ever survived a decade.

@linea_equilibrio
go-to: post_event
choose-if: disciplina >= 45
unavailable-subtitle: Holding both currents in one army takes a discipline the columns no longer have — the argument will be settled by other means.
on-arrival: unity += 2; disciplina += 2; political_capital -= 2

You refuse the choice the continent could not refuse, and spend real authority doing it: the militaristas get their operations and the mass-line cadres get their villages, and the leadership stands in the middle absorbing the friction that would otherwise become a split. It holds, this season, because you made it hold — and holding it is a cost, paid in the capital it takes to tell two convinced factions that they are both half right. Do it too many times and the center itself becomes a faction, and the loneliest one.

@el_ajusticiamiento
